Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Mild Cutaneous)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Classic malar rash post-sun exposure, positive ANA, absence of internal organ involvement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-046
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of a facial rash accompanied by severe anxiety requiring exclusion of an acute allergic reaction or systemic lupus crisis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a rapid-onset, burning facial rash following sun exposure. Immediate clinical review was necessary to differentiate a photosensitive autoimmune flare from acute facial cellulitis, severe contact dermatitis, or an impending systemic lupus flare prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My face suddenly turned bright red after I was in the garden today, it feels like it's burning, and I'm terrified my lupus is attacking my organs.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a photosensitive facial rash evolving rapidly over the past 4 hours following mild afternoon sun exposure. The rash is localized to the cheeks and nasal bridge, described as hot and tightly burning. The patient has a known history of positive ANA but previously asymptomatic. The abrupt cutaneous manifestation triggered acute panic regarding a severe, life-threatening systemic flare. Denies shortness of breath, chest pain, fever, joint swelling, or decreased urine output.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 66 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, distressed regarding facial appearance.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals a classic malar rash post-sun exposure, characterized by a well-demarcated erythematous plaque over the cheeks and nasal bridge, strictly sparing the nasolabial folds. The area is warm but not deeply indurated or fluctuant. No pustules or weeping crusts. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are normal. Abdomen is soft. Extremities show no peripheral edema. History confirms positive ANA, absence of internal organ involvement.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Not currently utilizing prescribed sunscreens or topical corticosteroids.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cutaneous Flare of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Mild Cutaneous) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Erysipelas/Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetric presentation strictly sparing the nasolabial folds, afebrile status, and lack of systemic toxicity.

Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Rash confined perfectly to the classic "butterfly" sun-exposed areas without typical eczematous weeping or severe pruritus.

Rosacea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Abrupt onset directly linked to acute sun exposure, and lack of telangiectasias or papulopustular lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, burning facial erythema, raising immediate concern for acute facial cellulitis or a severe allergic reaction. Triage correctly mandated urgent physical evaluation. Bedside assessment definitively established the classic morphology of a lupus malar rash, entirely excluding infectious or allergic etiologies. Furthermore, normal hemodynamics and lack of constitutional symptoms confirmed the absence of a systemic crisis (e.g., lupus nephritis). Providing immediate clinical reassurance and topical anti-inflammatory therapy safely de-escalated the patient's severe panic and entirely prevented an unnecessary emergency department presentation tonight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a mild topical corticosteroid (e.g., hydrocortisone 1%) for the face and recommended a cool compress.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a cool, damp cloth to the face for 10 minutes, followed by a thin layer of the steroid cream. By immediately cooling the skin, you will rapidly constrict the inflamed blood vessels, which will instantly stop the burning sensation and reduce the redness by tomorrow morning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP or rheumatology appointment in 7 days to discuss long-term photoprotection and potential initiation of hydroxychloroquine.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the redness spreads to your eyes and causes pain/vision loss, if you develop sharp chest pain when breathing in, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the lack of new systemic organ involvement; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This is a localized immune reaction in the skin triggered entirely by UV light. It causes burning and redness but does not mean your internal organs are suddenly failing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the rash breaks open into bleeding sores, or if you notice your ankles swelling and your urine turning a dark cola color.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sun caused your immune system to mistakenly attack the top layer of skin on your face. The steroid cream will suppress this local attack. There is no bacterial infection.
Actionable Steps: You must wear SPF 50+ sunscreen every single day, even when it is cloudy or if you are near a window. Avoid the sun entirely tomorrow.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Lupus facial rash (Malar rash). No signs of infection or internal organ damage. Use cool compresses and prescribed steroid cream. See GP in 7 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Use a cool compress and steroid cream for the burning. Avoid the sun and use SPF 50+ daily. Go to ED if you get sharp chest pain or a high fever.
